From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:24:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11273/11362] Measure the substrate vocabulary: 6 legal values,
 255 distinct aliases in use

Chasing one alias warn on exp7019 I found exp7021 and exp7022 draw the same warn
and was about to report this milestone as minting unallowlisted aliases. The base
rate refuted it: 14 distinct no-LLM aliases today against 30 on 08-29 and 14 on
09-03. Not a trend. Counting first is what stopped a non-regression being recorded
as one.

The number that does matter is all-time: 394 artifacts declare a *_no_llm
substrate using 255 distinct strings, against a discipline in CLAUDE.md that
defines six legal values. Every consumer switching on inference_substrate is
matching against a vocabulary it does not have.

It compounds with the hook finding from the previous hour: an uncontrolled
vocabulary, and no commit-time check on the population that produces it, because
conductor commits skip every hook.

Limits recorded with the measurement: counts key on file mtime rather than the
artifact's recorded run date and this project has a history of test runs
rewriting artifacts, so the daily split is approximate; only no_llm aliases were
counted; and none of this claims any individual alias is dishonest.

Not acted on. Adding 255 names to an allowlist is both forbidden and absurd; the
real options are operator-scale decisions about the discipline itself.

